Forwards are bilateral future-delivery contracts; futures are standardised with exchange margining and settlement rules.|Lock an asset purchase price at ₹100 for a future date.|A forward price lock guarantees the asset's future market price?|No|Yes|Only for commodities
A call grants a right to buy; a put a right to sell at a specified strike under contract terms.|Call strike ₹100, expiry price ₹120 gives ₹20 intrinsic payoff.|At expiry price ₹80, this call's payoff is…?|Zero|−₹20|₹80
Payoff is the contract outcome; profit also accounts for premiums and relevant costs.|Call payoff ₹20 less premium ₹7 gives ₹13 before other costs.|Profit before other costs is…?|₹13|₹20|₹27
Moneyness compares price with strike; intrinsic value is exercise value; time value is the remaining option price.|Call price ₹12 with intrinsic ₹8 has time value ₹4.|Time value is…?|₹4|₹12|₹20
European put-call parity links equivalent expiry payoffs, adjusted for dividends and funding assumptions.|For a non-dividend asset, C + PV(strike) = P + spot.|Parity requires comparable…?|Strike, expiry, and underlying|Only premium|Only buyer identity
Binomial pricing replicates payoffs across discrete states and uses no-arbitrage pricing under assumptions.|A one-step tree prices an option from up/down payoffs and funding.|Risk-neutral probabilities necessarily equal real-world forecast probabilities?|No|Yes|Only for calls
Black–Scholes prices European options under assumptions including specified diffusion, volatility, funding, and trading conditions.|Actual jumps and changing volatility can make model assumptions imperfect.|The model eliminates estimation and model risk?|No|Yes|Only if software is used
Greeks measure local sensitivity: delta price, gamma delta curvature, vega volatility, theta time, and rho rates.|Delta 0.5 implies about ₹0.50 option-price change for a small ₹1 underlying move.|The approximation remains exact for large moves?|No|Yes|Only for calls
Implied volatility is inferred from option prices using a model; realised volatility measures observed price variation.|Implied volatility rises even while recent realised volatility stays low.|They must be identical?|No|Yes|Only at-the-money
Smiles and skews describe implied volatility across strikes; term structures compare maturities.|Downside puts carry higher implied volatility than at-the-money options.|One constant volatility necessarily fits every strike?|No|Yes|Only on one date
Spreads combine strikes; straddles and strangles combine calls and puts; collars combine protection with an offsetting option sale.|A long straddle pays two premiums and benefits from sufficiently large moves.|A small move necessarily produces profit?|No|Yes|Only upward
Swaps exchange specified cash-flow streams for rate, currency, or commodity exposure.|A borrower swaps floating interest payments for fixed payments.|A swap cancels the original loan principal automatically?|No|Yes|Only if notional matches
CDS transfers specified reference-credit risk for premiums under contractual credit-event rules.|Protection may pay following a qualifying reference default.|Protection removes the buyer's counterparty risk?|No|Yes|Only for corporate bonds
Caps limit floating-rate upside exposure; floors protect downside; swaptions grant swap-entry rights.|A borrower buys a cap against rising reference rates.|A cap is generally an obligation to exercise?|No|Yes|Only above strike
Exotic payoffs depend on barriers, averages, or paths rather than only terminal price.|A barrier option expires if a specified level is crossed.|Same terminal price guarantees identical barrier-option payoff?|No|Yes|Only for calls
Structured notes embed derivatives in debt claims; advertised payoffs retain issuer and contractual risks.|A principal-protected note depends on the issuer honouring repayment.|Principal protection means no issuer credit risk?|No|Yes|Only at maturity
Hedging offsets existing risk; speculation takes risk; arbitrage seeks inconsistent equivalent-payoff prices.|An exporter sells foreign currency forward against expected receipts.|Evaluate the hedge with…?|The underlying exposure|Only derivative profit|Only forward notional
Margin and collateral support obligations; clearing changes risk channels but does not remove all default or liquidity risk.|A futures loss triggers cash margin calls before the underlying sale settles.|A successful long-term hedge can still create…?|Interim liquidity pressure|Guaranteed interim cash surplus|No collateral needs
Basis risk arises when the hedge and exposure do not move identically.|A fuel user hedges with a related but different commodity contract.|Residual risk is…?|Basis risk|Only accounting profit|Share dilution
